}

{
  "clause_name": "GENERAL_PROVISIONS",
  "issue": "The clause lacks clarity on the execution process and the binding nature of electronic signatures.",
  "suggested_language": "This Fifth Amendment may be executed in counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original, and all of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Signatures transmitted by facsimile or electronic mail in portable document format (PDF) shall be deemed valid and binding to the same extent as original signatures. IN WITNESS WHEREOF, the parties have caused this Fifth Amendment to be executed by their duly authorized representatives as of the dates set forth below.",
  "rationale": "The revised language clarifies that electronic signatures are as binding as original signatures, ensuring the enforceability of the agreement when executed in separate counterparts and transmitted electronically."
}

{
  "clause_name": "SERVICES",
  "issue": "The clause lacks clarity on the scope of services and the responsibilities of the Trustee.",
  "suggested_language": "All services provided by the Trustee shall be performed strictly in accordance with the written directions of the Sponsor or Named Fiduciary concerning the Plan's provisions, guidelines, and interpretations. The Trustee shall not have any discretionary authority or responsibility over (i) the management or administration of any Plan, or (ii) the investment of any Plan's assets. The Trustee's role shall be that of a directed trustee. The Trustee does not provide, and the Sponsor shall not interpret any services as, tax or legal advice. The Sponsor is responsible for obtaining its own legal and tax counsel for advice on Plan design and administration specific to its circumstances. Services may be rendered by the Trustee, its agents, subcontractors, or affiliates, with the Trustee remaining fully responsible for their performance as if performed by the Trustee itself. Certain services may be governed by separate contractual agreements, as explicitly noted herein.",
  "rationale": "This revision clarifies the Trustee's limited role and responsibilities, ensuring the Sponsor understands the need for independent legal and tax advice, and it emphasizes the Trustee's accountability for services performed by third parties, thereby protecting the client's interests."
}

{
  "clause_name": "LIABILITY",
  "issue": "The indemnification clause lacks clarity and specificity, potentially leading to disputes over interpretation.",
  "suggested_language": "8.1 Indemnification. Sponsor shall indemnify and hold harmless Trustee against any and all claims, liabilities, losses, damages, costs, or expenses (including reasonable attorneys' fees) arising out of or in connection with any third-party claims or regulatory proceedings to the extent such claims or proceedings result from any act or omission by any individual or person with respect to the Plans or Trust, except for those losses resulting from Trustee's negligence, bad faith, breach of fiduciary duty under ERISA, willful misconduct, or breach of this Trust Agreement. Conversely, Trustee shall indemnify and hold harmless Sponsor against any and all claims, liabilities, losses, damages, costs, or expenses (including reasonable attorneys' fees) arising out of or in connection with any third-party claims or regulatory proceedings to the extent such claims or proceedings result from Trustee\u2019s negligence, bad faith, breach of fiduciary duty under ERISA, willful misconduct, or breach of this Trust Agreement. Indemnification obligations extend to successors, parent companies, subsidiaries, affiliates, and their respective directors, officers, employees, contracted employees, representatives, plan administrators, and agents.",
  "rationale": "The revised language provides clearer definitions of indemnification obligations, specifying the scope of coverage and exceptions, thus reducing the risk of disputes and ensuring that both parties understand their responsibilities and liabilities."
}

{
  "clause_name": "SERVICES",
  "issue": "The clause lacks specificity regarding the scope and nature of services provided.",
  "suggested_language": "This Amendment No. 2 to the Security Services Customer Master Services Agreement (this \u201cAmendment\u201d), dated as of August 18, 2022 (\u201cAmendment Effective Date\u201d), is made by and between Dell USA L.P., for itself and its Subsidiaries (excluding Spyglass) (\u201cDell\u201d), and SecureWorks Inc., for itself and its Subsidiaries (\u201cSpyglass\u201d) (each a \u201cParty\u201d and collectively, the \u201cParties\u201d) to amend that certain Security Services Customer Master Services Agreement, dated July 7, 2015, that was entered into by and between the Parties (as amended, the \u201cAgreement\u201d or the \u201cMSA\u201d). The Parties agree that the services to be provided under this Amendment shall include, but not be limited to, the provision of security monitoring, threat analysis, and incident response services, as further detailed in Exhibit A attached hereto. Since the effective date of the Agreement, Spyglass and Dell have agreed to two amendments of the Agreement, which occurred on July 20, 2015 (Letter Agreement Re: Treatment of Existing Customer and Existing Projects) and November 3, 2017 (First Amendment Re: Fees with respect to NTT DATA Services, LLC), respectively. Capitalized terms used herein, but not defined herein, shall have the meanings given to such terms in the Agreement.",
  "rationale": "By specifying the types of services to be provided, the clause reduces ambiguity and ensures both parties have a clear understanding of the obligations, thereby minimizing potential disputes."
}

